r10imex — InterfaceModExp: modport EXPRESSION ports, GENERIC interface ports,
          const interface members

Build/compare one golden with

  SLANG_UHDM=<...>/build/slang-uhdm ./run.sh t1            # folded (default)
  SLANG_UHDM=<...>/build/slang-uhdm ./run.sh t1 -elabuhdm  # testflags
  ./all.sh                                                 # every t, both modes

run.sh uses the PINNED golden binary ~/git-repos/Surelog/build/bin/surelog —
the sv-tests copy writes a database uhdm-dump cannot restore.

STATUS (this block): t1 t3 t5 t6 BYTE-IDENTICAL both modes; t2/t4 down to the
tail-order + LowConn-loc residues of the UNMATCHED generic family; t7 is the
open residue file for that family (82 lines, all non-cmp — see below).

── t1  modport EXPRESSION ports (§ modportExprPort) ─────────────────────────
`modport A (output .P(r[3:0]), input .Q(x), R);` — each EXPRESSION port hangs
its connection expression on the io_decl as vpiExpr, in the DEF view AND in
the elaborated HOME view, never in a reference-style PORT view (which keeps
only the plain members' var Expr):
  .P(r[3:0]) -> a NAKED part_select: vpiName/vpiDefName = the base signal,
                vpiFullName <view>.<modport>.<io_decl>.<base>,
                vpiConstantSelect:1, 64-bit UINT Left/Right_range, NO actual;
  .Q(x)      -> a ref_obj on the same fullname rule whose vpiActual is the
                interface VARIABLE;
  .Q(2)      -> a bare 64-bit UINT constant (vpiConstType 9).
Plain members (R) get NO expr in these two views.

── t6  which instance the DEF view's ref binds ──────────────────────────────
Two instances of the same interface: work@I.A.Q.x binds work@top.i1.x — the
FIRST elaborated instance.  Each HOME view binds its own (i1 -> i1.x,
i2 -> i2.x).  Implemented as pendingModportExprRefs, drained by the first
non-port view of that definition.

── t3  `const` interface members ────────────────────────────────────────────
`const int x = 1` in an interface elaborates with vpiConstantVariable:1 (the
module-scope path already did this) and its init constant sizes at the
VARIABLE's width (int -> 32), not self-determined 64.  A `const` member ALSO
folds in one more place: an expression modport port `.Q(x)` naming it takes
the var's OWN init constant object as the io_decl Expr in the elaborated view
(InterfaceModExp), while the DEF view keeps the ref_obj.

── t2/t4/t5  § genericIfacePortNameMatch ────────────────────────────────────
THE KEY DISTINGUISHER of this cluster.  `module m (interface P);` names no
interface type, so Surelog resolves the port BY ITS OWN NAME against the
design's interface definitions.  t2 pairs both halves in one file — a single
interface `I`, `module sub (interface I)` and `module other (interface gen)`:

  MATCH (sub.I)      the port is an ordinary PLAIN (modport-less) interface
                     port.  DEF view: the bare logic_net is still minted, but
                     the port LowConn binds the DEF interface_inst and the
                     vpiTypedef is an interface_typespec NAMED BY THE PORT,
                     spanned at the port NAME (not the `interface` keyword).
                     ELAB view: NO logic_net at all; a nested interface_inst
                     at the INSTANCE line col 0, listed TWICE, LowConn ->
                     that view with the ref's port parent dropped; EVERY
                     modport is kept even when the connection names one
                     (`np u_np (inst.A)` — t4); the interface's variables are
                     NOT doubled (t5 — the doubling law is the unmatched form's).
  MISS  (other.gen)  the pre-existing unsupported_typespec / net-backed form.

t4 crosses matched/unmatched with named/positional; t5 adds the plain
(non-modport) connection to the matched half.

── t7  UNMATCHED generic ports: the OPEN residue file ───────────────────────
Four modules crossing plain/modport actuals with positional/named connections,
all named `gen` with no interface of that name.  Two laws landed from it:
  § genericIfacePortPositionalNoView — an unbound generic port materialises a
    nested port view only for a NAMED connection; positionally connected it
    keeps the bare net, and its LowConn is NAMED by the port and binds that
    net (the named forms bind the view/modport and carry no vpiName).
  the ELAB net's own unsupported_typespec back-points at the net.
RESIDUE, 82 dump lines, NONE cmp-relevant (uhdm-cmp reads neither locations
nor the weaklyReferenced tail order), ranked:
 1. (56 lines) a PLAIN-NAMED unbound generic port view SHARES the home view's
    variable objects; we mint fresh ones under the port path.  This is the
    other half of the boundToPlainGenericIfacePort doubling law: golden's home
    view lists the SAME objects twice and the port view lists those same
    objects.  Fixing it means teaching emitInterfaceInstance to reuse the home
    netMap for this one port form.
 2. (16 lines) the ELAB port's unsupported_typespec is created LATE by Surelog
    (after the sibling port views' member typespecs); we create it with the
    port.  Arena order only.
 3. (4 lines)  the LowConn ref loc of a NAMED unbound generic connection:
    golden anchors a MODPORT-qualified one at the PORT DECL name and a PLAIN
    one at the WHOLE `.gen(inst)` span; we use the formal-name token.  NOT
    changed here — OneNetModPortGeneric is the corpus witness for this form
    and a loc change there risks a byte-identity demotion for no cmp gain.
